# Supplementary material

Supplementary tables, data files and figures accompanying the PhD thesis:

**Ancient pathogen genomics of domestic small ruminants**
Louis L'Hôte
School of Genetics and Microbiology, Trinity College Dublin / School of Agriculture and Food Science, University College Dublin
Submitted [MONTH YEAR]

*[Replace the title, affiliation and date above with the versions on your title page.]*

---

## About

The thesis asks how the domestication of sheep and goats shaped the evolution of the pathogens that circulate in them — both those transmissible to humans and those confined to livestock. It comprises three research chapters, and this repository holds the supplementary material for each of them, one folder per chapter.

Material published elsewhere is duplicated here for convenience; the version of record is the journal supplementary information in each case, linked below.

---

## Repository structure

```
.
├── chapter2_pigsti/     Screening workflow for ancient animal remains
├── chapter3_brucella/   Neolithic Brucella melitensis genome
├── chapter4_capripox/   Ancient sheeppox virus genomes
└── README.md
```

### `chapter2_pigsti/`

Supplementary material for the development and application of PIGSTI (Pathogen anImal Genome Sequence ToolkIt), a modular workflow combining host species identification, microbial screening and pathogen authentication in ancient animal genomic datasets, applied to 953 archaeological livestock specimens.

| File | Description |
|---|---|
| `TableS1_samples.xlsx` | Specimen metadata: site, element, host species, period, accession, data source |
| `TableS2_benchmarking.xlsx` | Benchmarking results against simulated datasets for ten major ancient pathogens |
| `TableS3_candidate_hits.xlsx` | All 1,023 candidate pathogen hits with screening metrics |
| `TableS4_authenticated.xlsx` | The 109 authenticated detections, with authentication criteria |
| `FigS1–SX.pdf` | Supplementary figures |

*[Replace with your actual filenames. The pipeline itself is at [LINK TO PIGSTI REPOSITORY].]*

### `chapter3_brucella/`

Supplementary material for the recovery of a *Brucella melitensis* genome from the Neolithic sheep specimen Mentese6 (Menteşe Höyük, northwest Türkiye, 8007–7863 cal BP), and the calibration of the *B. melitensis* / *B. abortus* divergence.

| File | Description |
|---|---|
| `TableS1_samples.xlsx` | Screened ruminant specimens and sequencing summary |
| `TableS2_authentication.xlsx` | Screening and authentication metrics |
| `TableS3_reference_genomes.xlsx` | The 141 core *Brucella* genomes used for comparison |
| `TableS4_diagnostic_variants.xlsx` | Lineage-diagnostic variant genotyping |
| `TableS5_dating.xlsx` | BEAST model comparison and divergence estimates |
| `FigS1–SX.pdf` | Supplementary figures |

*[Replace with your actual filenames.]*

Published as: L'Hôte L, Light I, Mattiangeli V, Teasdale MD, Halpin Á, Gourichon L, Key FM, Daly KG (2024). An 8000 years old genome reveals the Neolithic origin of the zoonosis *Brucella melitensis*. *Nature Communications* 15:6132. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-50536-1

### `chapter4_capripox/`

Supplementary material for 21 ancient sheeppox virus genomes spanning the Late Bronze Age Eurasian steppe to Early Modern Europe, including the first pathogen genomes recovered from codicological material.

| File | Description |
|---|---|
| `TableS1_samples.xlsx` | Archaeological and codicological specimens screened |
| `TableS2_screening.xlsx` | Capripoxvirus screening results and E-values |
| `TableS3_genomes.xlsx` | Coverage and quality statistics for the 21 genomes |
| `TableS4_gene_inactivation.xlsx` | Gene inactivation states across ancient and modern genomes |
| `TableS5_dating.xlsx` | Temporal signal, clock model comparison and divergence estimates |
| `TableS6_rooting.xlsx` | Root posterior probabilities across clock models |
| `FigS1–SX.pdf` | Supplementary figures |

*[Replace with your actual filenames.]*

Preprint: L'Hôte L et al. (2026). [TITLE]. *bioRxiv*. https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.02.23.707469

